INT. GOLDEN LOTUS NAIL SALON - DAY

SUPER: "CHICAGO. 2:14 PM."

Pastel walls. Acetone in the air. A wall of polish bottles in every colour. A TV in the corner plays a muted boxing replay.

Two CUSTOMERS in the next chairs chat in Vietnamese. A KID in a school uniform does homework by the register.

THE WOMAN, 40s, sunglasses, tailored coat, sits at the manicure station. Her phone lies face down. Across the table, MARISOL, 30s, white smock, files her nails.

The file SCRAPES. A drier HUMS.

THE WOMAN
You do a lovely job.

MARISOL
(not looking up)
Thank you.

THE WOMAN
I mean it. Steady hands.

MARISOL
(smiling)
Practice.

She dips a brush. Paints a stripe of red.

INSERT - THE BOTTLE

Label: "Knockout."

BACK TO SCENE

THE WOMAN
Funny name.

MARISOL
It sells.

The Woman glances at the TV. The replay shows a boxer going down in the ninth.

THE WOMAN
You watch the fights?

MARISOL
My father did.

THE WOMAN
(carefully)
Did?

MARISOL
Every Saturday. He had a favourite. Danny Kessler.

The Woman goes still.

THE WOMAN
Kessler retired.

MARISOL
He was told to.

The brush keeps moving. Steady. Steady.

THE WOMAN
I'm not sure what you mean.

MARISOL
(mild)
My father bet the house on Danny. Round nine. Everybody knew it was fixed except him. He found out when the referee stopped it too early. A small man, tan suit, glasses. He looked at the floor so nobody could see his eyes.

(beat)

I was twelve. I remember my father holding the TV remote like it was a gun. I remember him saying one word, over and over. "Why." And then he stopped saying it.

The drier HUMS. The Kid turns a page.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
Years later I found the referee. Gambling debts. He'd been told to stop the fight, or lose his daughter's school fees. The man in the tan suit was a coward. But the man who told him? He was the coward's boss.

(beat)

That boss has a very good manicurist. She says he comes every second Tuesday.

THE WOMAN
(very still)
Does she.

MARISOL
She does.

The Woman's hand, flat on the table, does not move.

A DOOR BELL RINGS. Two men in dark jackets enter. They look around, then settle near the entrance.

The Woman watches them in the mirror.

THE WOMAN
You're quite sure of this.

MARISOL
I have his receipts. Every one.

THE WOMAN
You're not a manicurist.

MARISOL
I'm a very good manicurist.

She caps the bottle. CLICK.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
My name is Marisol Kessler.

The Woman takes off her sunglasses. Her eyes are old and tired.

THE WOMAN
Danny's daughter.

MARISOL
Yes.

THE WOMAN
Then you know my name.

MARISOL
Corinne Vale.

CORINNE
(a breath)
He deserved a better life than he got.

MARISOL
So did my father.

Corinne looks at the red nails, her hands under the lamp.

CORINNE
The men at the door. They're not mine.

MARISOL
No.

CORINNE
Yours?

Marisol nods slightly.

CORINNE (CONT'D)
(a faint laugh)
I came here for a manicure.

MARISOL
And you got one.

The TV roars. A crowd chanting a name that isn't Danny's.

Corinne's phone BUZZES on the table. She doesn't touch it.

It stops.

The Kid looks up from his homework.
